WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:10.552
Why has TSMC

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:12.512
become one of the most important companies in the world?

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:14.097
This tiny chip brought TSMC

00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:17.350
2.16 trillion NT dollars in annual revenue

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:18.893
accounted for nearly 10%

00:00:18.893 --> 00:00:21.438
of Taiwan’s GDP in 2023

00:00:21.438 --> 00:00:24.899
In July 2024, TSMC’s market capitalization of surpassed 1 trillion NT dollars

00:00:24.899 --> 00:00:26.317
making it the world’s second-largest semiconductor company

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:28.611
just behind Nvidia

00:00:28.611 --> 00:00:31.156
The semiconductor industry isn’t just competition between companies

00:00:31.156 --> 00:00:33.908
but can impact economy and national defense of a country

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:34.993
The most intensive game in modern tech

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:37.495
and even in politics &amp; economics

00:00:37.495 --> 00:00:39.414
is called the “chip war”

00:00:39.414 --> 00:00:41.332
Why is TSMC so important

00:00:41.332 --> 00:00:44.377
and why is it founded in Taiwan?

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:45.712
We have to shift our perspective

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:48.048
let this tiny chip answer the question

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:49.924
Why the third industrial revolution

00:00:49.924 --> 00:00:52.093
begin with this nanometer-sizes technology

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:58.516
Remember how the semiconductor industry rose?

00:00:58.516 --> 00:01:01.561
In 1959, Robert Noyce invented the mesa-structure IC

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.313
and two years earlier, in 1957

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:05.648
Jay Lathrop developed photolithography tech

00:01:05.648 --> 00:01:06.816
The two inventions

00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:09.152
meant the birth of “Chips”

00:01:09.152 --> 00:01:12.113
and established the foundation for mass production

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:14.157
hence started the chip era

00:01:14.157 --> 00:01:15.825
10 years later, in 1968

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:18.453
Noyce and Gordon Moore left Fairchild Semiconductor

00:01:18.453 --> 00:01:21.081
to establish Intel, focusing on memory chips

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.165
logic chips

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:23.750
and personal computers

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.336
This fueled the rapid growth of the semiconductor industry

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:27.796
But in 1968

00:01:27.796 --> 00:01:31.007
something very important to Taiwan happened

00:01:31.007 --> 00:01:33.009
To assess whether Taiwan

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:34.719
was ready for setting up factories

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:37.597
Texas Instruments (TI) sent two heavyweight figures

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:40.683
to meet with Taiwan’s Minister of Economic Affairs, Li Kwoh-ting

00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:41.643
These gentlemen were

00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:45.605
Mark Shepherd Jr., who would become TI’s CEO the following year

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:47.774
and Morris Chang

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:49.150
After this meeting

00:01:49.192 --> 00:01:52.320
Li Kwoh-ting spent the next 17 years

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.280
persuading Morris Chang to move to Taiwan

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.117
Looking back, this long-term recruitment mission

00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:58.326
proved to be invaluable

00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:00.912
You could say Li Kwoh-ting had remarkable foresight

00:02:00.912 --> 00:02:02.831
he recognized that transistors and semiconductors

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:04.749
were industries Taiwan had to invest in

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:07.544
But why on earth are transistors so important

00:02:07.544 --> 00:02:09.212
and why does demand for semiconductor production capacity

00:02:09.212 --> 00:02:10.839
get larger year by year?

00:02:10.839 --> 00:02:11.840
In the AI era

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.134
we all know only by aiming for more powerful computers

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:15.635
models with greater processing power

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:16.678
lower time costs

00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:18.721
and the ability to identify, compute and generate

00:02:18.721 --> 00:02:20.890
can we beat competitors in the market

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:22.016
It sounds complicated

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:23.726
yet it can be simply put

00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:26.479
For we need only two numbers 0 and 1

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:27.522
to store and precess all data

00:02:27.522 --> 00:02:30.608
with digital information

00:02:30.608 --> 00:02:32.610
If we only need to represent 0 and 1

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.279
then even electric lights can do that

00:02:34.279 --> 00:02:37.949
Let a lit lightbulb represent 1

00:02:37.949 --> 00:02:40.076
and an unlit one represent 0

00:02:40.076 --> 00:02:42.912
then a series of lit or unlit lightbulbs

00:02:42.912 --> 00:02:44.873
can form a sequence of data

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:46.166
such as Morse code

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:48.835
Of course, computers are not just on/off lights

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:50.545
They can run AI models

00:02:50.545 --> 00:02:52.463
to do all kinds of computing

00:02:52.463 --> 00:02:53.923
because they know how to “think”

00:02:54.048 --> 00:02:56.259
which means “logical operations”

00:02:56.259 --> 00:02:57.343
To make a computer think

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:59.304
doesn’t mean giving it a biological brain

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.472
like a human or any other intelligent lifeform

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.141
In fact even the large generative language models

00:03:03.141 --> 00:03:05.310
like GPT

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.144
when you break them down

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:08.938
there’s a supercomputer powering them

00:03:08.938 --> 00:03:10.732
What consist of this supercomputer

00:03:10.773 --> 00:03:12.942
are countless tiny processors

00:03:12.942 --> 00:03:15.111
At their core, the smallest units

00:03:15.111 --> 00:03:17.822
are vast numbers of tiny logic gates

00:03:18.615 --> 00:03:19.824
But what is a logic gate?

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.785
It’s like a tiny switch that operates based on rules

00:03:22.785 --> 00:03:24.662
If we increase the number of switches controlling the lightbulb

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:26.623
from a single switch to two

00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:28.833
and insert a “logit gate” in the middle

00:03:28.917 --> 00:03:30.835
you can play lots of tricks

00:03:30.835 --> 00:03:32.962
For example, we can design an “AND” gate

00:03:33.004 --> 00:03:34.964
to apply limit to the device

00:03:34.964 --> 00:03:37.884
Only if both gates are open will the lightbulb shine

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.135
If we replace it with an “OR” gate

00:03:39.135 --> 00:03:41.930
then either switched on will let lightbulb shine

00:03:42.347 --> 00:03:43.848
If you like something special

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:45.433
then you can use a “XOR” gate

00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:48.645
It lights up only when one switch is on and the other is off

00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:49.729
Let’s do a quick recap

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:52.774
Logic gates aren’t exclusive to transistors

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:55.944
We talked about how in 1937, before transistors existed

00:03:55.944 --> 00:03:57.820
Math genius Claude Shannon

00:03:57.862 --> 00:03:59.113
built a switching circuit with relays

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:01.783
that could do complex logic computing

00:04:01.824 --> 00:04:04.869
At first glance, logic gates seem to only handle simple sorting tasks

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:07.956
but these tiny units have evolved from simple switches

00:04:07.997 --> 00:04:10.875
to cores that can do basic logic operations

00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:13.628
If I rearrange these components

00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:15.797
to combine an AND gate with an XOR gate

00:04:15.838 --> 00:04:18.841
I create the simplest half adder

00:04:18.883 --> 00:04:20.843
that can calculate 0 + 0 = 0

00:04:20.885 --> 00:04:22.512
1 + 0 = 1

00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:24.681
0 + 1 = 1

00:04:25.139 --> 00:04:26.140
You might think, “Anyone can do this in their head!”

00:04:26.182 --> 00:04:27.350
Why do we need a computer for this?

00:04:27.642 --> 00:04:29.560
Hold on, take a step further

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:31.854
If we combine two half-adders,

00:04:31.896 --> 00:04:34.148
and add four AND gates for permutation

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.276
we get a simple binary multiplier

00:04:36.317 --> 00:04:41.114
that can multiply numbers from 0×0 to 3×3

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:44.450
From simple logical operations, we progressed to addition

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:45.952
and then multiplication

00:04:45.994 --> 00:04:46.828
and so on

00:04:46.828 --> 00:04:49.789
By combining enough logic gates in different arrangements

00:04:49.831 --> 00:04:53.084
we can perform division, square roots, and even calculus

00:04:53.084 --> 00:04:55.044
and various mathematical calculations

00:04:55.086 --> 00:04:56.254
Furthermore

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:58.548
Computers consists of these math operation units

00:04:58.589 --> 00:05:01.426
can execute virtually any task you can imagine

00:05:01.426 --> 00:05:04.387
Of course, we’re skipping a lot of details here

00:05:04.429 --> 00:05:05.513
But one major issue is:

00:05:05.555 --> 00:05:08.224
we can’t possibly design a new circuit

00:05:08.266 --> 00:05:10.143
for every single mathematical operation

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:12.061
For example, a dedicated circuit for squaring numbers

00:05:12.103 --> 00:05:13.855
or cubing numbers

00:05:13.855 --> 00:05:15.732
So, how to design a computer

00:05:15.773 --> 00:05:17.650
that can handle any kind of task?

00:05:17.692 --> 00:05:20.111
This ties back to what we discussed earlier

00:05:20.111 --> 00:05:21.863
about the Von Neumann model and Harvard architecture

00:05:21.904 --> 00:05:23.281
No time to elaborate now

00:05:23.281 --> 00:05:25.074
If you’d like to dive deeper

00:05:25.116 --> 00:05:27.327
leave a comment on the PanSci website

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:28.328
Now, back to our question

00:05:28.369 --> 00:05:31.539
We know anything can be done with logic gates

00:05:31.581 --> 00:05:32.749
but why can these gadgets

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:34.667
perform logical decision-making?

00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:37.337
Could there be a tiny alien operator

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:39.589
hiding inside?

00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:44.177
Well, inside every logic gate is a transistor

00:05:44.218 --> 00:05:46.846
We’ve covered the principle of transistors earlier

00:05:46.888 --> 00:05:50.183
It’s called ”triode”,the predecessor of vacuum tube

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:52.352
It works like a tiny water valve

00:05:52.393 --> 00:05:55.938
Using minor current to control whether a larger current can pass through

00:05:56.397 --> 00:05:57.857
If you forgot, that’s all right

00:05:57.899 --> 00:06:00.485
review it after watching this episode

00:06:00.485 --> 00:06:03.529
To sum up, a transistor is like a tiny water valve

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:05.615
It stays closed when there’s no input voltage

00:06:05.615 --> 00:06:07.992
and opens when voltage is applied

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:10.036
What happens if we connect two transistors together?

00:06:10.078 --> 00:06:10.912
you’ll find out

00:06:10.953 --> 00:06:13.539
only when both water valves are open at the same time

00:06:13.581 --> 00:06:15.625
can the water flow through

00:06:15.625 --> 00:06:17.710
This forms an AND gate

00:06:17.752 --> 00:06:21.130
Of course, other logic gates can be more complex

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:23.674
For example, a XOR gate looks like this

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:24.467
and so forth

00:06:24.467 --> 00:06:27.053
Transistors can build up all the logic gates

00:06:27.095 --> 00:06:29.722
and logic gates can accomplish all calculations

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:33.684
So transistors are the core of a CPU’s logic circuits

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:34.727
Nowadays

00:06:34.769 --> 00:06:37.146
whether it’s building faster CPUs

00:06:37.146 --> 00:06:38.564
or designing multi-core processors

00:06:38.606 --> 00:06:40.858
for parallel multitasking

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.528
we rely on increasing the number of transistors

00:06:43.528 --> 00:06:46.489
or developing newer, smaller chip structure

00:06:46.531 --> 00:06:48.533
In fact, since the founding of Intel

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:52.245
the world’s demand for computing power has only continued to grow

00:06:52.286 --> 00:06:54.664
till now, the race for more computing power

00:06:54.705 --> 00:06:56.249
shows no signs of slowing down

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:59.419
As the third industrial revolution swept across the globe

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:01.629
TSMC became one of its biggest beneficiaries

00:07:01.671 --> 00:07:03.423
bringing Taiwan onto the global stage

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.512
Why was TSMC founded in Taiwan

00:07:09.554 --> 00:07:11.431
instead of somewhere else?

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:12.807
From TSMC, Taiwan’s "Guardian Tech Giant,"

00:07:12.849 --> 00:07:14.600
to the entire Taiwanese semiconductor industry

00:07:14.642 --> 00:07:16.060
which forms the so-called “sillicon shield”

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:19.355
there are three key figures you must know

00:07:19.355 --> 00:07:21.232
They are Sun Yun-suan

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:23.818
Li Kwoh-ting and Morris Chang

00:07:23.818 --> 00:07:25.278
Sun Yun-suan and Li Kwoh-ting

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:28.239
both served as Taiwan’s Minister of Economic Affairs in the 1960s

00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:31.617
and were instrumental in shaping Taiwan’s economic and technological development

00:07:31.659 --> 00:07:35.830
Sun Yun-suan was the first director of TaiPower company in 1946

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:38.040
where he spearheaded efforts to rebuild Taiwan’s power grid

00:07:38.082 --> 00:07:39.876
This laid the foundation for Taiwan’s economic  development

00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:43.629
He also founded the Industrial Technology Research Institute (ITRI) in 1973

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:45.339
Moreover, in the 1970s,

00:07:45.381 --> 00:07:47.633
while Taiwan was promoting its Ten Major Construction Projects,

00:07:47.675 --> 00:07:51.137
he, along with Li Kwoh-ting and former National Tsing Hua University President Shu Shien-Siu,

00:07:51.179 --> 00:07:54.765
pushed for the establishment of Hsinchu Science Park.

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:58.144
Every one of these initiatives had a far-reaching impact on Taiwan’s economy

00:07:58.186 --> 00:08:00.229
When it comes to the establishment of ITRI and HSP

00:08:00.271 --> 00:08:02.148
Li Kwoh-ting also deserves a share of credit

00:08:02.148 --> 00:08:04.650
Yet Li’s another important credit

00:08:04.692 --> 00:08:07.069
was persuading Morris Chang to come to Taiwan

00:08:07.069 --> 00:08:08.738
The 17-year-long recruitment effort

00:08:08.779 --> 00:08:10.781
left a lasting impression on Morris Chang

00:08:10.781 --> 00:08:13.367
In November 2023, the Li Kwoh-ting Foundation

00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:15.328
presented the first LKT Award to

00:08:15.328 --> 00:08:17.330
none other than Morris Chang

00:08:17.371 --> 00:08:19.248
In his speech, Morris Chang stated

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.542
Without LKT, there would be no TSMC

00:08:21.542 --> 00:08:23.002
According to Morris Chang’s memoir

00:08:23.044 --> 00:08:25.713
he had three key encounters with LKT

00:08:25.713 --> 00:08:28.591
Firstly, after the initial meeting in 1968

00:08:28.633 --> 00:08:30.259
From then until 1980

00:08:30.301 --> 00:08:32.178
LKT made almost yearly trips to

00:08:32.178 --> 00:08:34.680
Texas Instruments (TI), where Morris Chang was working

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.015
tirelessly urging him to come to Taiwan

00:08:36.057 --> 00:08:38.267
He even took him to see the construction of Hsinchu Science Park

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:40.478
hoping to convince him to relocate

00:08:40.478 --> 00:08:43.272
The second encounter was in 1980, after the passing 

00:08:43.314 --> 00:08:45.024
of TI chairman Patrick Haggerty

00:08:45.024 --> 00:08:47.527
LKT reached out  again in 1982

00:08:47.568 --> 00:08:49.654
inviting Chang to become the director of ITRI in Taiwan

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:52.782
After careful consideration, Chang declined

00:08:52.782 --> 00:08:55.618
He recalled LKT’s frustration in his reply letter

00:08:55.618 --> 00:08:56.410
which read

00:08:56.452 --> 00:08:58.538
Why would you turn down such a great opportunity?

00:08:58.579 --> 00:09:00.957
The third and final time, Chang was finally persuaded

00:09:00.998 --> 00:09:04.252
and moved to Tawan to take up the position of ITRI director in 1985

00:09:04.252 --> 00:09:06.546
Just three weeks into the role

00:09:06.587 --> 00:09:09.549
LKT approached him with the idea of establishing a semiconductor company—TSMC

00:09:09.549 --> 00:09:11.968
He also convinced the government to invest in TSMC

00:09:12.009 --> 00:09:12.969
Morris Chang later elaborated

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.346
“The idea of founding TSMC and its business model

00:09:15.346 --> 00:09:17.014
was mine

00:09:17.014 --> 00:09:17.848
but executing the plan and

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:19.725
convincing the government to fund 50% of it

00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:21.227
was entirely LKT’s doing

00:09:21.269 --> 00:09:23.646
The business model Chang referred to

00:09:23.688 --> 00:09:25.731
was the pure-play foundry model

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:26.691
He foresaw that

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:29.318
fabless semiconductor companies would rise

00:09:29.318 --> 00:09:32.280
leading to a more specialized semiconductor industry

00:09:32.321 --> 00:09:34.949
Companies focused solely on chip design would appear

00:09:34.991 --> 00:09:38.661
while semiconductor foundries would grow into a massive market

00:09:38.703 --> 00:09:41.622
Morris Chang structured TSMC to focus solely on production, without designing chips

00:09:41.622 --> 00:09:43.416
maintaining a “collaborative” rather than a “competitive” relationship

00:09:43.416 --> 00:09:45.251
with other companies

00:09:45.251 --> 00:09:46.544
This strategy set the stage

00:09:46.544 --> 00:09:47.878
for what we’ll discuss in the next episode—

00:09:47.878 --> 00:09:49.964
the ongoing battle between TSMC and Samsung

00:09:50.006 --> 00:09:52.550
The impact of this strategy will become clear

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:55.094
Fast forward to the 2000s

00:09:55.136 --> 00:09:56.721
the landscape of semiconductor manufacturing in Asia

00:09:56.762 --> 00:09:57.847
are largely

00:09:57.888 --> 00:10:00.975
a competition between TSMC and Samsung

00:10:01.017 --> 00:10:03.269
Samsung was no easy competitor

00:10:03.311 --> 00:10:05.479
Since Apple’s first-generation iPhone chips

00:10:05.479 --> 00:10:08.566
the choice was Samsung instead of TSMC

00:10:08.608 --> 00:10:11.569
As transistors continued to shrink

00:10:11.611 --> 00:10:13.779
approaching the 20-nanometer node

00:10:13.821 --> 00:10:16.407
We found that further miniaturization became increasingly difficult

00:10:16.449 --> 00:10:18.784
Any smaller transistor brings leakage  

00:10:18.784 --> 00:10:19.744
and higher power consumption

00:10:19.744 --> 00:10:21.454
Logic gates become invalid too

00:10:21.495 --> 00:10:22.580
Faced with this challenge

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:24.540
how did TSMC respond?

00:10:24.582 --> 00:10:26.959
And who will carry forward Moore’s Law?

00:10:27.001 --> 00:10:27.627
In the next episode

00:10:27.627 --> 00:10:30.338
We’re entering the world of 3D transistors

00:10:30.379 --> 00:10:32.548
and witness the Taiwan-Korea semiconductor battle

00:10:32.548 --> 00:10:35.843
explore the true reason behind Taiwan’s dominance in the semiconductor industry

00:10:35.885 --> 00:10:37.428
Stay tuned to our channel

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.680
See you next time

